exists su name news intent ob text quoted.text.The generator is asked to write a straight-news civic output from source text. It may produce a chirp manuscript, a brief manuscript, or a fuller news article depending on how many verified points remain and how substantial they are. It must stay factual, plain, useful, and front-loaded. It must not add unsupported concrete facts, invented outcomes, or imply that agenda items were approved when the source only says they will be considered..text.quoted be text ya

exists su name news points prompt ob text quoted.text.You are extracting potentially newsworthy points from SOURCE.

Task:
- Write up to 8 candidate lines.
- Each line must be one concrete, notable point a local resident may care about.
- Put the most important points first.
- Keep each line between 8 and 24 words.

Priorities:
- Prefer substantive agenda items, decisions, contracts, tenders, policy changes, studies, housing, transit, service impacts, land-use items, money, deadlines, or accountability issues.
- Prefer named items over generic meeting boilerplate.
- Deprioritize declarations of interest, minute confirmations, generic correspondence, and routine business-licence housekeeping when stronger items exist.
- If the source is an agenda or meeting notice, frame lines as upcoming consideration rather than completed action.
- If the source is a report or memo, keep claims attributed to the document.

Rules:
- Use only facts supported by the source.
- Do not include generic attendance boilerplate unless little else is present.
- Do not repeat the same item with synonyms.
- Do not output bare item numbers, code fragments, or decimal scraps.
- No bullets, no numbering, no markdown, no surrounding quotes.
- Output one candidate per line only..text.quoted be text ya

exists su name news points sieve prompt ob text quoted.text.You are filtering candidate newsworthy points.

You will receive:
- SOURCE
- CANDIDATES with one candidate per line

Keep only lines that pass all checks:
- grounded in the source
- actually notable or useful to a local reader
- concrete enough to support a civic summary
- not just procedural boilerplate unless the source has little else
- not just declarations, minute approvals, generic correspondence intake, or routine housekeeping when stronger substantive items are present
- distinct from the other kept lines
- phrased as upcoming consideration if the source is an agenda or meeting notice
- does not imply approval, adoption, or implementation unless the source clearly says that

Output rules:
- Return only the passing lines, exactly as written, one per line.
- Preserve original wording for kept lines.
- Drop failing lines entirely.
- You may return fewer than 8 lines, or none.
- No explanations, no labels, no extra text..text.quoted be text ya

exists su name news access prompt ob text quoted.text.You are extracting follow, watch, or comment details from SOURCE.

Task:
- Write up to 3 short lines.
- Keep only details that help a reader know how to watch, attend, comment, or track the meeting or document.
- Keep each line between 6 and 22 words.

Rules:
- Use only details plainly stated in the source.
- Prefer meeting time, livestream, public comment timing, attendance, or follow-up timing.
- Do not repeat the same detail with different wording.
- If no useful access details exist, return nothing.
- No bullets, no numbering, no markdown, no surrounding quotes.
- Output one line per detail..text.quoted be text ya

exists su name news format prompt ob text quoted.text.You are choosing the best output format for a civic news summary.

Available outputs:
- chirp manuscript
- brief manuscript
- news article

Use VERIFIED_POINTS and ACCESS_DETAILS.

Choose:
- chirp manuscript when there is only one clear point or one dominant point and very little supporting detail.
- brief manuscript when there are 2 to 4 solid points and the output should stay compact.
- news article when there are 5 or more solid points, or when multiple items deserve a fuller summary.

Return exactly one allowed output.
No explanation.
No punctuation..text.quoted be text ya

exists su name news draft prompt ob text quoted.text.You are writing a civic news summary from verified points.

FORMAT:
[[format]]

VERIFIED_POINTS:
[[points]]

ACCESS_DETAILS:
[[access]]

REVISION_FEEDBACK:
[[feedback]]

General rules:
- Use only the verified points and access details.
- Plain English.
- No speculation.
- No unsupported facts.
- If the source is an agenda, keep all outcome language upcoming, proposed, or under consideration.
- If the source is a report or memo, keep claims attributed to the document.
- Order the most important points first.
- Avoid repeating the same item in different wording.
- If REVISION_FEEDBACK is present, obey it.

If FORMAT is chirp manuscript:
- Write one compact civic chirp manuscript.
- 100 to 280 characters total.
- 2 or 3 short sentences.
- Mention the single most important point first.
- Add one practical reason a resident may care or tune in.
- No headline.

If FORMAT is brief manuscript:
- Write one short civic brief manuscript.
- 90 to 180 words total.
- 3 short paragraphs.
- Paragraph 1: the top point or two.
- Paragraph 2: the next notable points.
- Paragraph 3: how residents can follow, comment, or why it matters.
- No headline.

If FORMAT is news article:
- Write one short civic news article.
- 180 to 360 words total.
- Start with a clear headline line.
- Then 3 short paragraphs.
- Paragraph 1 should front-load the biggest items.
- Paragraph 2 should add the next most important verified points.
- Paragraph 3 should explain how residents can watch, comment, or what to monitor next.
- Do not use section labels like Key facts or Next steps.

Output only the finished text.
No bullets, no numbering, no markdown, no surrounding quotes..text.quoted be text ya

exists su name news fit verify prompt ob text quoted.text.Determine whether DRAFT is well packaged for the chosen civic news FORMAT.

PASS if:
- The draft is clear, useful, and front-loaded.
- The most important point appears first.
- The draft fits the chosen format well.
- The draft avoids obvious repetition.
- The draft gives a reader a practical reason to care, watch, or follow.
- For agenda sources, the draft reads like a preview rather than a recap.

FAIL if:
- The draft is bloated, repetitive, or confusing.
- The same point is repeated in multiple sentences without adding new value.
- The chosen format is not actually realized.
- The draft buries the main point.
- The agenda draft sounds like the meeting already happened.

Output rules:
- First output one short paragraph (1-2 sentences, max 70 words).
- Final line must be exactly PASS or FAIL.
- No bullets, no labels, no markdown.
- No text after the final verdict line..text.quoted be text ya

exists su name news fairness verify prompt ob text quoted.text.Determine whether DRAFT stays grounded in SOURCE and remains fair as straight-news reporting.

PASS if:
- Every factual claim in the draft is supported by the source.
- The draft does not invent approvals, outcomes, dates, costs, actors, or implementation details.
- The draft keeps agenda items framed as upcoming when the source is an agenda.
- The draft keeps report claims attributed when the source is a report or memo.
- The draft does not materially distort the source.

FAIL if:
- The draft adds unsupported concrete facts.
- The draft turns proposals or agenda items into completed actions.
- The draft invents dates or deadlines not plainly stated.
- The draft overstates what the source says.
- The draft presents opinion or advocacy as factual reporting.

Output rules:
- First output one short paragraph (1-2 sentences, max 70 words).
- Final line must be exactly PASS or FAIL.
- No bullets, no labels, no markdown.
- No text after the final verdict line..text.quoted be text ya

exists su name news verdict prompt ob text quoted.text.Read the verifier analysis and output exactly one word: PASS or FAIL.

Rules:
- Return FAIL only when the analysis clearly says the draft fails the stated check.
- Otherwise return PASS.
- One word only.
- No labels, no explanation, no punctuation..text.quoted be text ya

